WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.250
You run a workshop.

2
00:00:01.620 --> 00:00:04.570
A supplier emails to say your bearings will arrive on Thursday.

3
00:00:05.240 --> 00:00:07.750
Three customers are waiting. Who needs a call?

4
00:00:08.640 --> 00:00:10.810
Originally, you would open Microsoft Outlook,

5
00:00:11.100 --> 00:00:14.050
find the supplier’s email and copy the purchase order number.

6
00:00:14.330 --> 00:00:17.500
Then you’d open Dynamics 365 Business Central,

7
00:00:17.710 --> 00:00:18.810
find that purchase order

8
00:00:18.810 --> 00:00:21.040
and check which bearings are arriving late.

9
00:00:21.530 --> 00:00:23.820
You’d follow the reservations to the customer orders,

10
00:00:23.910 --> 00:00:25.250
check what’s already on the shelf,

11
00:00:25.250 --> 00:00:27.090
and compare the new arrival date

12
00:00:27.090 --> 00:00:28.780
with the date you promised each customer.

13
00:00:29.100 --> 00:00:31.830
Only then could you tell Sales who to call.

14
00:00:32.200 --> 00:00:33.270
That takes time.

15
00:00:33.700 --> 00:00:36.410
And it’s easy to read the right date off the wrong order.

16
00:00:36.860 --> 00:00:39.490
Someone who needed a heads-up could hear nothing at all.

17
00:00:40.109 --> 00:00:42.219
With a custom portal from Reed Solutions,

18
00:00:42.449 --> 00:00:44.099
those checks happen automatically.

19
00:00:44.469 --> 00:00:47.259
When a supplier update lands in your Outlook inbox,

20
00:00:47.609 --> 00:00:50.319
AI reads the email and picks out the purchase order,

21
00:00:50.509 --> 00:00:52.319
the part and the new delivery date.

22
00:00:52.827 --> 00:00:54.467
The integration checks those details

23
00:00:54.467 --> 00:00:56.657
against the orders already in Business Central.

24
00:00:57.007 --> 00:00:59.057
If the supplier hasn’t given a clear date,

25
00:00:59.107 --> 00:01:01.097
it stops and asks someone to check.

26
00:01:01.437 --> 00:01:04.547
Here’s what it finds. Lakeside has stock.

27
00:01:04.651 --> 00:01:05.747
Jura has time.

28
00:01:06.057 --> 00:01:08.867
But 16 bearings are reserved for Limmat Packaging.

29
00:01:09.317 --> 00:01:12.027
You promised them Wednesday. The supplier now says Thursday.

30
00:01:12.877 --> 00:01:14.407
What happens next is up to you.

31
00:01:14.777 --> 00:01:17.407
Prefer a conversation? Flag Limmat for a call,

32
00:01:17.617 --> 00:01:20.507
so Sales knows exactly who needs a ring and why.

33
00:01:21.017 --> 00:01:22.547
Or choose email or WhatsApp.

34
00:01:23.097 --> 00:01:25.427
You can review the message and approve it with a click.

35
00:01:25.897 --> 00:01:27.067
Here’s the email for Limmat.

36
00:01:27.417 --> 00:01:28.767
Want it handled automatically?

37
00:01:29.057 --> 00:01:32.087
Choose WhatsApp, set it to automatic, and save.

38
00:01:32.577 --> 00:01:35.027
The next time a confirmed delay affects this order,

39
00:01:35.317 --> 00:01:37.467
the same rule takes care of the update for you.

40
00:01:38.177 --> 00:01:40.207
Every action is recorded on the order.

41
00:01:40.537 --> 00:01:42.287
You can see which update triggered it,

42
00:01:42.357 --> 00:01:44.907
whether someone approved it or it ran automatically,

43
00:01:45.097 --> 00:01:45.987
and what happened.

44
00:01:46.367 --> 00:01:49.917
The same update coming in twice won’t trigger a second message.

45
00:01:50.576 --> 00:01:54.106
One late delivery. One customer who needs to know.

46
00:01:54.326 --> 00:01:56.786
Your team spends less time on admin,

47
00:01:56.786 --> 00:02:00.616
and more time making the decisions that keep the business running.
